    {
        "content": "<p>fitzgen requested <a href=\"https://github.com/dicej\">dicej</a> for a review on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705\">PR #14705</a>.</p>",
        "id": 631381953,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791909206
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>fitzgen requested <a href=\"https://github.com/orgs/bytecodealliance/teams/wasmtime-core-reviewers\">wasmtime-core-reviewers</a> for a review on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705\">PR #14705</a>.</p>",
        "id": 631381956,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791909206
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"memo\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4dd\" role=\"img\" title=\"memo\">:memo:</span> alexcrichton submitted <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705#pullrequestreview-5506896847\">PR review</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I might recommend using a version field rather than a <code>size</code> field (or maybe both?) -- I've had some good experience with that in the past over in <code>wit-bindgen</code>. With just a <code>size</code> field then it might still be possible to read uninitialized padding bytes for example where trailing <code>bool</code>s might not resize the structure due to padding but are otherwise uninitialized. With a version field where documentation can be added for \"this field was added in this version\" that might help with that problem.</p>\n<p>IIRC I've seen other C libraries have a version field as well, such as with the curl library.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 631383312,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791909526
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>fitzgen <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705#issuecomment-6150492166\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705\">PR #14705</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Good idea, I didn't think about trailing padding.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 631384317,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791909770
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>alexcrichton <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705#issuecomment-6150570077\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705\">PR #14705</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If it's helpful, <a href=\"https://github.com/curl/curl/blob/2b348649c68c1cd8cc1d055487f2fb2b59a353cb/include/curl/curl.h#L3113-L3249\">this is what curl does</a></p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 631385511,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791910030
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>fitzgen updated <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705\">PR #14705</a>.</p>",
        "id": 631391169,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791911174
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>fitzgen <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705#issuecomment-6150921214\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705\">PR #14705</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Updated to include a version field as well.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 631391259,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791911194
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"thumbs up\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f44d\" role=\"img\" title=\"thumbs up\">:thumbs_up:</span> alexcrichton submitted <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705#pullrequestreview-5507299968\">PR review</a>.</p>",
        "id": 631393303,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791911681
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"speech balloon\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4ac\" role=\"img\" title=\"speech balloon\">:speech_balloon:</span> alexcrichton created <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14705#discussion_r4261740454\">PR review comment</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I think this might technically be unsound given MIRI-style rules because this is aserting that all of <code>config</code> is valid initialized memory for <code>WasmBenchConfig</code> which isn't the case for padding bytes an added fields. I think the only 100% safe way to model this would be to preserve the raw pointer under-the-hood and go to fields through raw pointers (not through <code>&amp;WasmBenchConfig</code>).</p>\n<p>Another possible example is if the latest version is version 2, but this is given version 1. In that case the accessor of <code>cache</code> below currently goes through <code>&amp;WasmBenchConfig</code> but that pointer is actually invalid for version 1 because version 2 presumably added more fields so we'd be tricking rustc into thinking there's more fields than there actually are.</p>\n<p>This is a pretty minor concern though and may not actually end up causing any issues in practice.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 631393306,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1791911681
    },
